Cell[TextData[{
 "Figure 7.2  Cumulative flight energy along each species' ",
 StyleBox["centroid",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " path over the year. This is explicitly a ",
 StyleBox["lower bound",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " on the true annual energy: it integrates the ~",
 "24,115",
 " km centroid displacement, whereas a tracked Arctic tern actually flies \
~70,900 km (Egevang et al. 2010) \[LongDash] roughly three times as far \
\[LongDash] so its real annual fuel bill dwarfs the centroid-path figure \
shown here."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"cf97e4ec-a32c-4efc-9bf5-7098de35fa46"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["A note on rigour. ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "These are ",
 StyleBox["first-order estimates",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " \[LongDash] a point-mass bird in still air in steady level flight, with \
literature default coefficients. They are meant for ",
 StyleBox["cross-species comparison",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", not as physiology: real flight involves wind, climb, gliding (the swift \
especially), variable payload, and individual variation the model does not \
capture. Treat the numbers as order-of-magnitude, and the ranking between \
species as more trustworthy than any single absolute value."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{4, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {6, 10}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.85, 0.6, 0.15],
 Background->RGBColor[
  1., 0.97, 0.88],ExpressionUUID->"f241e369-035c-4ed6-88cb-ec795ecb2869"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["8.  Finding the way: the magnetic landscape", "Section",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontSize->30,
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"edb2b66b-badc-4815-89f4-0e7d921e7d1f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["How does a bird know where it is?",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Navigation over an ocean with no landmarks is the deepest puzzle of \
migration, and the answer is emphatically ",
 StyleBox["multimodal",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ": migratory birds draw on a ",
 StyleBox["sun compass",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", a ",
 StyleBox["star compass",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", polarised-light cues at dawn and dusk, learned landmarks, olfaction, and \
\[LongDash] the part Wolfram Language lets us actually compute \[LongDash] \
the ",
 StyleBox["Earth's magnetic field",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", used both as a ",
 StyleBox["compass",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (direction) and, more controversially, as a ",
 StyleBox["map",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (position). The compass sense is an ",
 StyleBox["inclination",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " compass: birds read the dip angle between the field and the horizon rather \
than its polarity (Wiltschko & Wiltschko 1972). For the broader mechanism see \
Mouritsen (2018)."
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"e8c5f364-a866-43f6-9cd1-46f994225af2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["What we can compute.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Wolfram Language ships the IGRF/WMM geomagnetic models through ",
 StyleBox["GeomagneticModelData",
  FontFamily->"Courier"],
 ", so we can evaluate the field everywhere along each flyway and ask what \
magnetic ",
 StyleBox["landscape",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " the birds traverse. The map below shows magnetic inclination (dip) as a \
global backdrop with the three centroid tracks laid over it:"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"132f4ae9-fe97-4b1a-9d8e-9e82709e9bb0"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
(* Magnetic inclination (dip) along a track, via the built-in model.
   Abbreviated from wolfram/geomag.wls. *)
dip[lat_, lon_] := QuantityMagnitude @ GeomagneticModelData[
   GeoPosition[{lat, lon}], \"MagneticDipAngle\"];
GeoRegionValuePlot[ ... ]   (* dip backdrop *)
trackDip = dip @@@ Transpose[{tern[[All, 1]], tern[[All, 2]]}];\
\>", "Input",
 CellMargins->{{60, 30}, {6, 6}},
 FontSize->11,ExpressionUUID->"aa199986-c5a2-43fd-a9d5-6072babdaeee"],

